<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<assembly xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v1" manifestVersion="1.0">

  <!-- ===================================================================== -->
  <!-- BetterMagnifier application manifest                                  -->
  <!-- ===================================================================== -->

  <!--
    The version here is written by release-please, like the ones in
    src\Version.h and version.txt - the marker on the version line is what it
    matches. Four components because assemblyIdentity requires them; the tool
    rewrites the leading three and leaves the trailing .0 alone.

    tools\check-version.ps1 asserts this agrees with src\Version.h, because a
    manifest that quietly disagreed with the resource is precisely the drift
    9d6838d had to go and clean up.
  -->
  <assemblyIdentity type="win32"
                    name="BetterMagnifier"
                    processorArchitecture="*"
                    version="0.2.0.0" /> <!-- x-release-please-version -->

  <!--
    ── WHERE administrator privilege is actually declared ──

    Not here. Writing requestedExecutionLevel into this file breaks the build
    with "manifest authoring error c1010001: Values of attribute level not
    equal in different manifest snippets": MSBuild generates its own manifest
    fragment with asInvoker, and mt.exe cannot merge two different level
    values.

    The right place is the vcxproj: <Link><UACExecutionLevel>RequireAdministrator.
    That way the generated manifest is correct from the start and there is no
    merge conflict.

    WHAT ELEVATION BUYS US:
      The elevated process runs at a higher integrity level. Low-level hooks
      (WH_KEYBOARD_LL / WH_MOUSE_LL) can see and swallow input from windows at
      a LOWER integrity level than themselves. At normal integrity we could
      not swallow the Windows Magnifier's Win+Plus shortcut; elevated, the
      odds are better.

    WHAT IT DOES NOT BUY US — IMPORTANT:
      Elevation is not UIAccess. APIs that require UIAccess, such as
      MagSetFullscreenTransform, still will not work; that needs a signed exe,
      installation in a trusted location, and uiAccess="true".

    THE COST:
      - A UAC prompt on every launch
      - Drag and drop from non-elevated applications does not work (UIPI)
      - Auto-start with Windows (an HKCU Run entry) silently fails for an
        elevated process; a Task Scheduler task running "with highest
        privileges" is needed instead
  -->

  <!-- ── Supported Windows versions ── -->
  <!--
    Without this block Windows runs the application in "compatibility mode"
    and downgrades the behaviour of some APIs. The Windows 10/11 GUID is
    required.
  -->
  <compatibility xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:compatibility.v1">
    <application>
      <supportedOS Id="{8e0f7a12-bfb3-4fe8-b9a5-48fd50a15a9a}" />
    </application>
  </compatibility>

  <!--
    DPI awareness is deliberately NOT declared in the manifest.
    main.cpp sets it in code via
    SetProcessDpiAwarenessContext(PER_MONITOR_AWARE_V2). Declaring it in both
    places conflicts; the manifest wins and the code-side call fails silently.
  -->

</assembly>
